    "source": "Utimaco Atalla AT1000 Command Reference Manual v8.60 (Response 11D)",
    "markdownDoc": {
      "es": "# Command 11D - Generate a Working Key\n\n- **Command ID:** `11D`\n- **Title:** Generate a Working Key\n- **PDF Pages:** 718 - 720\n\n---\n\n### Description\n\nCommand 11D can generate a PIN Encryption Key, Data Encryption Key, or MAC Key. In addition to being encrypted under a specified MFK variant (1, 2 or 3), the generated key will also be encrypted under variant 0 of a Key Exchange Key (KEK).\n\n### Key Management Compatibility\n\n| Key Type | Default Setting | MFK Type |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 🌸 AKB | Enabled | 3DES, AES |\n| 🟪 Variant | Disabled | 3DES, AES |\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```\n<11D#Key Usage Working Key#Key Usage KEK#Key Exchange Key#[Key Length#]>\n```\n\n### Response Syntax\n\n```\n<21D#Working Key#Working Key)#Check Digits#>[CRLF]\n```\n\n### Command Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Command identifier | 3 | 11D |\n| 1 | Key Usage - Working Key | 1 | The variant applied to the MFK when encrypting the generated Working Key. This field contains a number in the range of 1 - 3, where: 1 = PIN Encryption Key (KPE) 2 = Data Encryption Key (KD) 3 = Message Authentication Key (KMAC) |\n| 2 | Key Usage - Key Exchange Key | 1 | The variant applied to the MFK when the Key Exchange Key cryptogram was created. This field contains a number, either 0 or 5. |\n| 3 | Key Exchange Key | 16, 32 | The Key Exchange Key encrypted under the variant specified in field 2 of the MFK. This field contains 16 or 32 hexadecimal characters, or a key table location. |\n| 4 | [Key Length#] | [1] | This field is optional. If it is not present, a 1key-3DES key will be generated. This field contains a number, either 1 or 2, where: 1 = Generate a 1key-3DES (single-length) key 2 = Generate a 2key-3DES (double-length) key |\n\n### Response Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 3 | 21D |\n| 1 | Working Key | 16, 32 | The generated Working Key encrypted under the variant specified in field 1 of the MFK. This field contains 16 hexadecimal characters. It will contain 32 hexadecimal characters if field 4 contains the number 2. |\n| 2 | Working Key | 16, 32 | The generated Working Key encrypted under the KEK. This field contains 16 hexadecimal characters. It will contain 32 hexadecimal characters if field 4 contains the number 2. |\n| 3 | Check Digits | 4, 6 | The Working Key check digits are the leftmost four hexadecimal characters that result from encrypting zeros under the Working Key. If option 88 is enabled, the leftmost six check digits are returned. |\n\n### Usage Notes\n\n- Encrypt the Key Exchange Key under variant zero of the MFK.\n- The length of the Key Exchange Key must be equal-to or greater-than the length of the Working Key.\n- The Key Exchange Key cannot be a replicated 1key-3DES (single-length) key.\n\n### Examples\n\nExamples\n\nWorking Key Variant: 3\n\nKey Exchange Key Variant: 5\n\nKey Length: 1\n\nClear-text Key Encryption Key (KEK): 0000 1111 2222 3333\n\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<11D#3#5#784DDF5F89FB9EBF#>\n```\n\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<21D#8E9FD3F747107BD1#B2A3B78CB1FE15AC#7FEA#>\n```\n\nWorking Key Variant: 3\n\nKey Exchange Key Variant: 5\n\nKey Length: 2\n\nClear-text Key Encryption Key (KEK): 0000 1111 2222 3333 4444 5555 6666 7777.\n\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<11D#3#5#784DDF5F89FB9EBFCDCB224AE77756B2#2#>\n```\n\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<21D#25166617EC743AB125166617EC743AB1#F8C6AB5B46CFD570F8C6AB5B46CFD570#D5D4#>\n```\n\n",
      "en": "# Command 11D - Generate a Working Key\n\n- **Command ID:** `11D`\n- **Title:** Generate a Working Key\n- **PDF Pages:** 718 - 720\n\n---\n\n### Description\n\nCommand 11D can generate a PIN Encryption Key, Data Encryption Key, or MAC Key. In addition to being encrypted under a specified MFK variant (1, 2 or 3), the generated key will also be encrypted under variant 0 of a Key Exchange Key (KEK).\n\n### Key Management Compatibility\n\n| Key Type | Default Setting | MFK Type |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 🌸 AKB | Enabled | 3DES, AES |\n| 🟪 Variant | Disabled | 3DES, AES |\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```\n<11D#Key Usage Working Key#Key Usage KEK#Key Exchange Key#[Key Length#]>\n```\n\n### Response Syntax\n\n```\n<21D#Working Key#Working Key)#Check Digits#>[CRLF]\n```\n\n### Command Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Command identifier | 3 | 11D |\n| 1 | Key Usage - Working Key | 1 | The variant applied to the MFK when encrypting the generated Working Key. This field contains a number in the range of 1 - 3, where: 1 = PIN Encryption Key (KPE) 2 = Data Encryption Key (KD) 3 = Message Authentication Key (KMAC) |\n| 2 | Key Usage - Key Exchange Key | 1 | The variant applied to the MFK when the Key Exchange Key cryptogram was created. This field contains a number, either 0 or 5. |\n| 3 | Key Exchange Key | 16, 32 | The Key Exchange Key encrypted under the variant specified in field 2 of the MFK. This field contains 16 or 32 hexadecimal characters, or a key table location. |\n| 4 | [Key Length#] | [1] | This field is optional. If it is not present, a 1key-3DES key will be generated. This field contains a number, either 1 or 2, where: 1 = Generate a 1key-3DES (single-length) key 2 = Generate a 2key-3DES (double-length) key |\n\n### Response Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 3 | 21D |\n| 1 | Working Key | 16, 32 | The generated Working Key encrypted under the variant specified in field 1 of the MFK. This field contains 16 hexadecimal characters. It will contain 32 hexadecimal characters if field 4 contains the number 2. |\n| 2 | Working Key | 16, 32 | The generated Working Key encrypted under the KEK. This field contains 16 hexadecimal characters. It will contain 32 hexadecimal characters if field 4 contains the number 2. |\n| 3 | Check Digits | 4, 6 | The Working Key check digits are the leftmost four hexadecimal characters that result from encrypting zeros under the Working Key. If option 88 is enabled, the leftmost six check digits are returned. |\n\n### Usage Notes\n\n- Encrypt the Key Exchange Key under variant zero of the MFK.\n- The length of the Key Exchange Key must be equal-to or greater-than the length of the Working Key.\n- The Key Exchange Key cannot be a replicated 1key-3DES (single-length) key.\n\n### Examples\n\nExamples\n\nWorking Key Variant: 3\n\nKey Exchange Key Variant: 5\n\nKey Length: 1\n\nClear-text Key Encryption Key (KEK): 0000 1111 2222 3333\n\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<11D#3#5#784DDF5F89FB9EBF#>\n```\n\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<21D#8E9FD3F747107BD1#B2A3B78CB1FE15AC#7FEA#>\n```\n\nWorking Key Variant: 3\n\nKey Exchange Key Variant: 5\n\nKey Length: 2\n\nClear-text Key Encryption Key (KEK): 0000 1111 2222 3333 4444 5555 6666 7777.\n\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<11D#3#5#784DDF5F89FB9EBFCDCB224AE77756B2#2#>\n```\n\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<21D#25166617EC743AB125166617EC743AB1#F8C6AB5B46CFD570F8C6AB5B46CFD570#D5D4#>\n```\n\n"
    }
