      "es": "# Command 1223 - HSM TCP/IP Socket Information\n\n- **Command ID:** `1223`\n- **Title:** HSM TCP/IP Socket Information\n- **PDF Pages:** 1352 - 1353\n\n---\n\n### Description\n\nCommand 1223 returns the number of sockets on the HSM available for new connections, the total number of sockets available for reconnect from the host system that sent this command, and the number of reconnect sockets.\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```\n<1223#[[Port#]NIC#]]>\n```\n\n### Response Syntax\n\n```\n<2223#Remaining Sockets#Total Sockets#Reconnect Sockets#>[CRLF]\n```\n\n### Command Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Command identifier | 4 | 1223 |\n| 1 | [Port#] | [4, 5] | This field is optional. When present, it contains an HSM port number which has been assigned to either the PORT_ASCII, PORT_MANAGEMENT, or PORT_STATUS. When field 2 =2, this field contains an HSM port number which has been assigned to either the PORT_ASCII_2, PORT_MANAGEMENT_2, or PORT_STATUS_2. When this field is not present, socket information for the port number the host application used to send this command is returned. When this field is not present, and the command is sent to the serial port, socket information for PORT_ASCII is returned. |\n| 2 | [NIC#] | [1] | This field is optional. It can only be present when field 1 exists. When present, it contains the NIC number (1, or 2). When this field is not present NIC 1 is used. |\n\n### Response Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 4 | 2223 |\n| 1 | Remaining Sockets | 1 -2 | This field contains number of available sockets. |\n| 2 | Total Sockets | 1 - 2 | This field contains the total number of sockets defined for the port number. |\n| 3 | Reconnect Sockets | 1 - 2 | This field contains the number of sockets available for reconnect based on the port number. When RECONNECT=no is present in the config.prm file, this field will contain the uppercase letter X. |\n\n### Usage Notes\n\n- Option 23 applies to this command. All of the other options that can be defined by command 101 do not apply to this command.\n- The HSM’s Activity Log provides socket connection information.\n- If a specific host establishes 10 new socket connections with the HSM, it has 10 reconnect sockets available. Reconnect sockets are used by the HSM only when all available sockets are in use.\n- Here is an example of when reconnect sockets are used. Assume that the HSM is configured for 16 sockets, and host A has 11 sockets open on the HSM, and host B has 3 sockets open on the HSM; 14 of the 16 possible sockets are in use on the HSM. Host B loses power which leaves 3 sockets on the HSM in a hung state. Host B is immediately restarted and attempts to reconnect to the HSM, the first 2 socket open requests from Host B will be granted as new socket connects by the HSM, whereas the third socket open request from Host B will be granted as a reconnect socket by the HSM. Any attempts by a host other than A or B to connect with the HSM will fail, as all available sockets are in use (Host A is using 11, and Host B is using 5, three of which are hung). At this point, Host A has 11 reconnect sockets available, and Host B has 2 reconnect sockets available. Assume that another application on Host A now tries to establish 12 socket connections to the HSM, only the first 11 will be granted as reconnect sockets, the 12th open request will fail, as there are no available sockets on the HSM and Host A has used all of its reconnect sockets. Assume another application on Host B now tries to establish 4 socket connections to the HSM, only the first 2 will be granted as reconnect sockets, the last 2 open requests will fail, as there are no available sockets and Host B has used all of its reconnect sockets. After the KEEP_ALIVE_TIME expires (default is 20 minutes) the HSM detects that the 3 sockets originally established with Host B are hung, they are deleted from the HSM, and the number of available sockets is set to 3.\n\n### Examples\n\nExample\n\nSockets available: 15\n\nTotal sockets: 16\n\nReconnect sockets: 1\n\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<1223#>\n```\n\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<2223#15#16#1#>\n```\n\n",
