    "source": "Utimaco Atalla AT1000 Command Reference Manual v8.60 (Command 312)",
    "markdownDoc": {
      "es": "# Command 312 - Generate PAN Binding Token\n\n- **Command ID:** `312`\n- **Title:** Generate PAN Binding Token\n- **PDF Pages:** 1113 - 1114\n\n---\n\n### Description\n\nCommand 312 generates a PAN binding token. PCI prohibits changing the PAN when translating or verifying a PIN block. This restriction creates a problem when the incoming PIN block was encrypted using a tokenized PAN. This command solves the issue by creating a cryptographic binding between the \"real\" PAN and the tokenized value via the AES CMAC algorithm. The resulting blob is a PAN Binding token.\n\n### Key Management Compatibility\n\n| Key Type | Default Setting | MFK Type |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 🌸 AKB | Disabled | AES |\n| 🟪 Variant | Enabled | 3DES |\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```\n<312#Binding Version#Algorithm#PAN Binding Key#Tokenized PAN#PAN#>\n```\n\n### Response Syntax\n\n```\n<412#PAN Binding Token#Check Digits#>\n```\n\n### Command Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Command ID | 3 | 312 |\n| 1 | Binding Version | 1 | The format of the PAN binding token. Legal Characters: 1 |\n| 2 | Algorithm | 1 | This field indicates the MAC algorithm to use: 2 = AES CMAC Legal Characters: 2 |\n| 3 | PAN Binding Key | 74, 90, 106, 110 | The CMAC Key encrypted under the MFK. Headers Supported: 3BJN*00B, 3BJG*00B, where * = E, N, or S. Legal Characters: Printable ASCII |\n| 4 | Tokenized PAN | 12-19 | The tokenized PAN value. If only \"compact\" PIN blocks are supported (e.g. ISO-0 and ISO-3), then this can be either the 12 digit ANSI PAN or the full tokenized card number. If ISO-4 may be supported, include the full card number. Legal Characters: 0-9 |\n| 5 | PAN | 12-19 | The value of the actual PAN. If only \"compact\" PIN blocks are supported (e.g. ISO-0 and ISO-3), then this can be either the 12 digit ANSI PAN or the full tokenized card number. If ISO-4 may be supported, include the full card number. Legal Characters: 0-9 |\n\n### Response Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 3 | 412 |\n| 1 | PAN Binding Token | 46-60 | The generated PAN Binding Token. Legal Characters: 0-9, A-F |\n| 2 | Check Digits | 10 | The check digits are the leftmost ten digits from CMAC of a zero block. Legal Characters: 0-9, A-F |\n\n### Usage Notes\n\n- This command should be enabled in the issuing or tokenization environments rather than an on-line environment where PIN blocks are being processed. If the environment lacks sufficient compensating controls to limit the use of this command, this command can be enabled as a counted command.\n- It is possible that a system may need to translate between two different tokenization schemes. While this command is written assuming that the second PAN input is the actual user PAN, there is nothing in the HSM architecture that prevents translating between two PAN tokens as long as the two are bound together. AKB AES MFK Example The AES Master File Key is: 2ABC3DEF45670189 98107645FED3CBA2 0123456789ABCDEF FEDCBA9876543210 check digits = B9BCAB7B62. See “AES Key (256-bit)” on page 1374 for component values.\n- The clear text CMAC Key = 00001111222233334444555566667777, check digits = FEF900C20E The CMAC Key in AKB format 3BJNE00B,2555E8880E29821FC38300C621DBBD39,DA8E3CD9A847FFC0CFD438A1E087BF62\n- Tokenized PAN = 1234567890123456789\n- PAN = 9876543210987654 The command looks like this: <312#1#2#3BJNE00B,2555E8880E29821FC38300C621DBBD39,DA8E3CD9A847FFC0CFD438A1E08 7BF62#1234567890123456789#9876543210987654#> The HSM returns this response: <412#01191234567890123456789169876543210987654FB6719C26E4FF844B#FEF900C20E#>\n\n",
      "en": "# Command 312 - Generate PAN Binding Token\n\n- **Command ID:** `312`\n- **Title:** Generate PAN Binding Token\n- **PDF Pages:** 1113 - 1114\n\n---\n\n### Description\n\nCommand 312 generates a PAN binding token. PCI prohibits changing the PAN when translating or verifying a PIN block. This restriction creates a problem when the incoming PIN block was encrypted using a tokenized PAN. This command solves the issue by creating a cryptographic binding between the \"real\" PAN and the tokenized value via the AES CMAC algorithm. The resulting blob is a PAN Binding token.\n\n### Key Management Compatibility\n\n| Key Type | Default Setting | MFK Type |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 🌸 AKB | Disabled | AES |\n| 🟪 Variant | Enabled | 3DES |\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```\n<312#Binding Version#Algorithm#PAN Binding Key#Tokenized PAN#PAN#>\n```\n\n### Response Syntax\n\n```\n<412#PAN Binding Token#Check Digits#>\n```\n\n### Command Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Command ID | 3 | 312 |\n| 1 | Binding Version | 1 | The format of the PAN binding token. Legal Characters: 1 |\n| 2 | Algorithm | 1 | This field indicates the MAC algorithm to use: 2 = AES CMAC Legal Characters: 2 |\n| 3 | PAN Binding Key | 74, 90, 106, 110 | The CMAC Key encrypted under the MFK. Headers Supported: 3BJN*00B, 3BJG*00B, where * = E, N, or S. Legal Characters: Printable ASCII |\n| 4 | Tokenized PAN | 12-19 | The tokenized PAN value. If only \"compact\" PIN blocks are supported (e.g. ISO-0 and ISO-3), then this can be either the 12 digit ANSI PAN or the full tokenized card number. If ISO-4 may be supported, include the full card number. Legal Characters: 0-9 |\n| 5 | PAN | 12-19 | The value of the actual PAN. If only \"compact\" PIN blocks are supported (e.g. ISO-0 and ISO-3), then this can be either the 12 digit ANSI PAN or the full tokenized card number. If ISO-4 may be supported, include the full card number. Legal Characters: 0-9 |\n\n### Response Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 3 | 412 |\n| 1 | PAN Binding Token | 46-60 | The generated PAN Binding Token. Legal Characters: 0-9, A-F |\n| 2 | Check Digits | 10 | The check digits are the leftmost ten digits from CMAC of a zero block. Legal Characters: 0-9, A-F |\n\n### Usage Notes\n\n- This command should be enabled in the issuing or tokenization environments rather than an on-line environment where PIN blocks are being processed. If the environment lacks sufficient compensating controls to limit the use of this command, this command can be enabled as a counted command.\n- It is possible that a system may need to translate between two different tokenization schemes. While this command is written assuming that the second PAN input is the actual user PAN, there is nothing in the HSM architecture that prevents translating between two PAN tokens as long as the two are bound together. AKB AES MFK Example The AES Master File Key is: 2ABC3DEF45670189 98107645FED3CBA2 0123456789ABCDEF FEDCBA9876543210 check digits = B9BCAB7B62. See “AES Key (256-bit)” on page 1374 for component values.\n- The clear text CMAC Key = 00001111222233334444555566667777, check digits = FEF900C20E The CMAC Key in AKB format 3BJNE00B,2555E8880E29821FC38300C621DBBD39,DA8E3CD9A847FFC0CFD438A1E087BF62\n- Tokenized PAN = 1234567890123456789\n- PAN = 9876543210987654 The command looks like this: <312#1#2#3BJNE00B,2555E8880E29821FC38300C621DBBD39,DA8E3CD9A847FFC0CFD438A1E08 7BF62#1234567890123456789#9876543210987654#> The HSM returns this response: <412#01191234567890123456789169876543210987654FB6719C26E4FF844B#FEF900C20E#>\n\n"
    },
