          "es": "The data used to calculate the MAC. The length must be a multiple of 16 hexadecimal characters. The data block consists of transaction specific data elements generated by the terminal and smart card. To determine the contents of this field, refer to the Application Cryptogram Generation section of the EMV Book 2 Security and Key Management and also consult with your smart card personalization and smart card application vendors. It is the responsibility of the host application to right pad the data per the relevant standard. The HSM does not perform any additional padding or check for the existence of padding. Legal Characters: 0-9, A-F The height value used for EMV-Tree derivation. This field is present only when the EMV-Tree derivation type is used (field 1 contains a value of 9). This field contains the value 8 or 16, or it can be empty. If this field is empty and field 1 contains a value of 9, the height value of 8 is used. Legal Characters: 8, 16 The clear Initialization Vector used for EMV-Tree derivation. This field is present only when the EMV-Tree derivation type is used (field 1 contains a value of 9). If this field is empty and field 1 contains a value of 9, an IV of 32-bytes of 0 is used. Legal Characters: 0-9, A-F The index value used for EMV-Tree derivation. The index specifies the byte location of the key that is exclusive-OR (EXOR) with the application transaction counter (ATC) coefficient. - An index value of zero indicates the HSM will XOR the leftmost byte of the key with the ATC coefficient. - An index value of 7 indicates the HSM will XOR the rightmost byte of the key with the ATC coefficient. - If the key is double-length, the index value is applied to both halves of the double length key. This field is present only when the EMV-Tree derivation type is used (field 1 contains a value of 9). If this field is empty and field 1 contains a value of 9, the index value of 7 is used. Legal Characters: 0-7",
