    "markdownDoc": {
      "es": "# Command 7C - Symmetric Key Size\n\n- **Command ID:** `7C`\n- **Title:** Symmetric Key Size\n- **PDF Pages:** 508 - 510\n\n---\n\n### Description\n\nCommand 7C allows auditors and individuals in other capacities to find out the actual length of a key wrapped in an AKB block. This feature is useful for checking compliance with key size mandates. CAUTION: Enabling this command allows anyone with access to the HSM (such as insiders, malware) to determine the keys that are most vulnerable to cryptanalysis. Users are cautioned that single-DES keys are vulnerable to key search at trivial cost, so allowing an attacker to pinpoint vulnerable keys is a significant risk. Utimaco recommends careful analysis of the security tradeoffs of enabling this command.\n\n### Key Management Compatibility\n\n| Key Type | Default Setting | MFK Type |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 🌸 AKB | Disabled | 3DES, AES |\n| 🟪 Variant | Enabled | 3DES |\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```\n<7C#Key#>\n```\n\n### Response Syntax\n\n```\n<8C#Length#Check Digits#>\n```\n\n### Command Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Command identifier | 2 | 7C |\n| 1 | Key | Variable | The symmetric key encrypted under the MFK. This command should support all symmetric working key headers (version 1 and version 3). Legal Characters: Printable ASCII |\n\n### Response Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 2 | 8C |\n| 1 | Length in bytes |  | The length of the key in bytes. For example, a single-DES key will return a value of 8. TDES keys are 16 or 24. AES keys are 16, 24, or 32. HMAC keys may vary. Legal Characters: 0-9 |\n| 2 | Check digits |  | The check digits of the symmetric key.  TDES key: check digits are the leftmost 6 digits calculated by encrypting 0 with the key.  AES key: check digits are the leftmost 10 digits of the CMAC of all zeros.  HMAC key: check digits are the leftmost 14 digits of the SHA-256 hash of the KMAC key. Legal Characters: 0-9, A-F |\n\n### Usage Notes\n\n- When option EE is enabled, the AKB length of the AES or HMAC Working Key supplied in command field 1 may be 110 characters. AKB 3DES MFK Example 3key-3DES Master File Key = 2ABC 3DEF 4567 0189 9810 7645 FED3 CBA2 0123 4567 89AB CDEF, check digits = B196. See Table A-2, 3key-3DES Key (Triple-Length) for component values.\n- Clear-text DES working key = 3333 3333 3333 3333, check digits = ADC67D\n- Working key in AKB format = 1PUNE000,648CC44390726074BA90F7D2EB760D1D3BCDE456C1FA7915,4B8EF70DF76A5C9D The command looks like this: <7C#1PUNE000,648CC44390726074BA90F7D2EB760D1D3BCDE456C1FA7915,4B8EF70DF76A5C9D #> The HSM returns this response: <8C#8#ADC67D#> AKB AES MFK Example The AES Master File Key is: 2ABC3DEF45670189 98107645FED3CBA2 0123456789ABCDEF FEDCBA9876543210, check digits = B9BCAB7B62. See “AES Key (256-bit)” on page 1374 for component values.\n- Clear-text DES working key = 3333333333333333, check digits = ADC67D\n- Working key in AKB format = 3PUNE000,09F0C618F35FCDB8729715D61092B92B9554B066CD04C8DC,C9E6FCE5D702F7F4 The command looks like this: <7C#3PUNE000,09F0C618F35FCDB8729715D61092B92B9554B066CD04C8DC,C9E6FCE5D702F7F4 #> The HSM returns this response: <8C#8#ADC67D#>\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> **CAUTION:** Enabling this command allows anyone with access to the HSM (such as insiders, malware) to determine the keys that are most vulnerable to cryptanalysis. Users are cautioned that single-DES keys are vulnerable to key search at trivial cost, so allowing an attacker to pinpoint vulnerable keys is a significant risk. Utimaco recommends careful analysis of the security tradeoffs of enabling this command.\n\n",
      "en": "# Command 7C - Symmetric Key Size\n\n- **Command ID:** `7C`\n- **Title:** Symmetric Key Size\n- **PDF Pages:** 508 - 510\n\n---\n\n### Description\n\nCommand 7C allows auditors and individuals in other capacities to find out the actual length of a key wrapped in an AKB block. This feature is useful for checking compliance with key size mandates. CAUTION: Enabling this command allows anyone with access to the HSM (such as insiders, malware) to determine the keys that are most vulnerable to cryptanalysis. Users are cautioned that single-DES keys are vulnerable to key search at trivial cost, so allowing an attacker to pinpoint vulnerable keys is a significant risk. Utimaco recommends careful analysis of the security tradeoffs of enabling this command.\n\n### Key Management Compatibility\n\n| Key Type | Default Setting | MFK Type |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 🌸 AKB | Disabled | 3DES, AES |\n| 🟪 Variant | Enabled | 3DES |\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```\n<7C#Key#>\n```\n\n### Response Syntax\n\n```\n<8C#Length#Check Digits#>\n```\n\n### Command Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Command identifier | 2 | 7C |\n| 1 | Key | Variable | The symmetric key encrypted under the MFK. This command should support all symmetric working key headers (version 1 and version 3). Legal Characters: Printable ASCII |\n\n### Response Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 2 | 8C |\n| 1 | Length in bytes |  | The length of the key in bytes. For example, a single-DES key will return a value of 8. TDES keys are 16 or 24. AES keys are 16, 24, or 32. HMAC keys may vary. Legal Characters: 0-9 |\n| 2 | Check digits |  | The check digits of the symmetric key.  TDES key: check digits are the leftmost 6 digits calculated by encrypting 0 with the key.  AES key: check digits are the leftmost 10 digits of the CMAC of all zeros.  HMAC key: check digits are the leftmost 14 digits of the SHA-256 hash of the KMAC key. Legal Characters: 0-9, A-F |\n\n### Usage Notes\n\n- When option EE is enabled, the AKB length of the AES or HMAC Working Key supplied in command field 1 may be 110 characters. AKB 3DES MFK Example 3key-3DES Master File Key = 2ABC 3DEF 4567 0189 9810 7645 FED3 CBA2 0123 4567 89AB CDEF, check digits = B196. See Table A-2, 3key-3DES Key (Triple-Length) for component values.\n- Clear-text DES working key = 3333 3333 3333 3333, check digits = ADC67D\n- Working key in AKB format = 1PUNE000,648CC44390726074BA90F7D2EB760D1D3BCDE456C1FA7915,4B8EF70DF76A5C9D The command looks like this: <7C#1PUNE000,648CC44390726074BA90F7D2EB760D1D3BCDE456C1FA7915,4B8EF70DF76A5C9D #> The HSM returns this response: <8C#8#ADC67D#> AKB AES MFK Example The AES Master File Key is: 2ABC3DEF45670189 98107645FED3CBA2 0123456789ABCDEF FEDCBA9876543210, check digits = B9BCAB7B62. See “AES Key (256-bit)” on page 1374 for component values.\n- Clear-text DES working key = 3333333333333333, check digits = ADC67D\n- Working key in AKB format = 3PUNE000,09F0C618F35FCDB8729715D61092B92B9554B066CD04C8DC,C9E6FCE5D702F7F4 The command looks like this: <7C#3PUNE000,09F0C618F35FCDB8729715D61092B92B9554B066CD04C8DC,C9E6FCE5D702F7F4 #> The HSM returns this response: <8C#8#ADC67D#>\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> **CAUTION:** Enabling this command allows anyone with access to the HSM (such as insiders, malware) to determine the keys that are most vulnerable to cryptanalysis. Users are cautioned that single-DES keys are vulnerable to key search at trivial cost, so allowing an attacker to pinpoint vulnerable keys is a significant risk. Utimaco recommends careful analysis of the security tradeoffs of enabling this command.\n\n"
    },
