    "markdownDoc": {
      "es": "# Command 9A - HSM DIAGTEST\n\n- **Command ID:** `9A`\n- **Title:** HSM DIAGTEST\n- **PDF Pages:** 575 - 576\n\n---\n\n### Description\n\nCommand 9A – This command performs a cryptographic self-test on the HSM.\n\n### Key Management Compatibility\n\n| Key Type | Default Setting | MFK Type |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 🌸 AKB | Enabled | 3DES, AES |\n| 🟪 Variant | Enabled | 3DES |\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```\n<9A#DIAGTEST#Algorithm#[Perform All Flag]#>\n```\n\n### Response Syntax\n\n```\n<AA#Result#>[CRLF]\n```\n\n### Command Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Command identifier | 2 | 9A |\n| 1 | DIAGTEST | 8 | This field contains the word DIAGTEST. |\n| 2 | Algorithm | 1- 5 | This field contains a decimal value which specifies the test to be performed, where: 0 = All tests 1 = 3DES known answer test 2 = Random Number Generation test 3 = RSA known answer test 4 = MD5 known answer test 5 = SHA-1 known answer test 6 = SHA-2 known answer test 7 = Kernel/Image test 8 = AES known answer test 9 = HMAC_SHA256 known answer test 10 = HMAC-SHA1 known answer test 11 = SHA512 known answer test 12 = AES GCM and CMAC known answer test 13 = ECC algorithm tests 14 = KDF known answer test Leading zeros are allowed, but not required. For example, 0 - 12 and 00000 - 00012 are valid values. |\n| 3 | [Perform All Flag] | 0, 1, 2 | This field is only relevant when field 2 = 0. When field 2 = 0 and this field is empty, the HSM will perform algorithm tests 1, 2, and 4 - 12, and 14. When field 2 = 0 and this field contains a value in the range of 1 - 99, the HSM will perform all algorithm tests. |\n\n### Response Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 2 | AA |\n| 1 | Result | 2, Variable | When the test passes, this field contains the word OK. When a test fails this field contains the name of the failed test. |\n\n### Usage Notes\n\n- The test results are recorded in the system log.\n\n### Examples\n\nExample\n\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<9A#DIAGTEST#0#1#>\n```\n\n```\n<AA#OK#>\n```\n\n",
      "en": "# Command 9A - HSM DIAGTEST\n\n- **Command ID:** `9A`\n- **Title:** HSM DIAGTEST\n- **PDF Pages:** 575 - 576\n\n---\n\n### Description\n\nCommand 9A – This command performs a cryptographic self-test on the HSM.\n\n### Key Management Compatibility\n\n| Key Type | Default Setting | MFK Type |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 🌸 AKB | Enabled | 3DES, AES |\n| 🟪 Variant | Enabled | 3DES |\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```\n<9A#DIAGTEST#Algorithm#[Perform All Flag]#>\n```\n\n### Response Syntax\n\n```\n<AA#Result#>[CRLF]\n```\n\n### Command Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Command identifier | 2 | 9A |\n| 1 | DIAGTEST | 8 | This field contains the word DIAGTEST. |\n| 2 | Algorithm | 1- 5 | This field contains a decimal value which specifies the test to be performed, where: 0 = All tests 1 = 3DES known answer test 2 = Random Number Generation test 3 = RSA known answer test 4 = MD5 known answer test 5 = SHA-1 known answer test 6 = SHA-2 known answer test 7 = Kernel/Image test 8 = AES known answer test 9 = HMAC_SHA256 known answer test 10 = HMAC-SHA1 known answer test 11 = SHA512 known answer test 12 = AES GCM and CMAC known answer test 13 = ECC algorithm tests 14 = KDF known answer test Leading zeros are allowed, but not required. For example, 0 - 12 and 00000 - 00012 are valid values. |\n| 3 | [Perform All Flag] | 0, 1, 2 | This field is only relevant when field 2 = 0. When field 2 = 0 and this field is empty, the HSM will perform algorithm tests 1, 2, and 4 - 12, and 14. When field 2 = 0 and this field contains a value in the range of 1 - 99, the HSM will perform all algorithm tests. |\n\n### Response Parameters\n\n| Field | Name | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 2 | AA |\n| 1 | Result | 2, Variable | When the test passes, this field contains the word OK. When a test fails this field contains the name of the failed test. |\n\n### Usage Notes\n\n- The test results are recorded in the system log.\n\n### Examples\n\nExample\n\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<9A#DIAGTEST#0#1#>\n```\n\n```\n<AA#OK#>\n```\n\n"
    },
